"A small spark jumped from my finger.": "7693c3b5efdf",
"A thundercloud is a very busy place. Warm, moist air shoots upward, and cold air and rain fall back down. Inside the cloud, drops of water and bits of ice are thrown around and rub against each other. This pulls electric charge apart. Scientists still do not understand every detail, but the result is clear: positive charge collects near the top of the cloud, and negative charge collects near the bottom.": "6114769fa917",
"At last the air cannot hold it back, and the charge jumps. That giant spark is lightning. It can jump inside one cloud, from one cloud to another, or between the cloud and the ground. All over the world, lightning strikes the ground about 100 times every second.": "1d0e050f7a29",
"Lightning and thunder happen at the same moment, but they do not reach you together. Light travels about 300,000 kilometers a second, so you see the flash at once. Sound travels only about 340 meters a second. If you count the seconds between the flash and the boom, you can tell how far away the lightning was. Every 3 seconds is about 1 kilometer.": "9dc1d5599c05",
"Lightning can strike a tall tree.": "1f1afb3921d7",
"Lightning is a kind of electricity.": "5ebfbc221d8b",
"Lightning is extremely hot. It heats the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius in an instant. The air swells so fast that it bursts outward, like a small explosion. The sound of that burst is thunder. So thunder is not clouds crashing together. It is the sound of air being heated by lightning.": "59751dc506df",
"Lightning lit up the whole sky.": "41c34ccf257e",
"On a hot summer afternoon, the sky can change very quickly. A white cloud grows tall and turns dark at the bottom. Then there is a flash, and a few seconds later, a boom. For thousands of years people wondered what was happening up there. Today we know that it is electricity.": "97eb6f9bf00d",
"On a hot summer afternoon, the sky can change very quickly. A white cloud grows tall and turns dark at the bottom. Then there is a flash, and a few seconds later, a boom. For thousands of years people wondered what was happening up there. Today we know that it is electricity. A thundercloud is a very busy place. Warm, moist air shoots upward, and cold air and rain fall back down. Inside the cloud, drops of water and bits of ice are thrown around and rub against each other. This pulls electric charge apart. Scientists still do not understand every detail, but the result is clear: positive charge collects near the top of the cloud, and negative charge collects near the bottom. Opposite charges pull on each other. The negative charge at the bottom of the cloud also pulls positive charge toward the ground just below. But air stands between them, and air does not let electricity pass easily. So the charge builds and builds. In some large storms, the push behind it reaches several million volts. At last the air cannot hold it back, and the charge jumps. That giant spark is lightning. It can jump inside one cloud, from one cloud to another, or between the cloud and the ground. All over the world, lightning strikes the ground about 100 times every second. Lightning is extremely hot. It heats the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius in an instant. The air swells so fast that it bursts outward, like a small explosion. The sound of that burst is thunder. So thunder is not clouds crashing together. It is the sound of air being heated by lightning. Lightning and thunder happen at the same moment, but they do not reach you together. Light travels about 300,000 kilometers a second, so you see the flash at once. Sound travels only about 340 meters a second. If you count the seconds between the flash and the boom, you can tell how far away the lightning was. Every 3 seconds is about 1 kilometer.": "1462618651cd",
